## The idea

Bulb i is toggled once for every round that **divides i**. Divisors come in pairs (d and i/d), so most bulbs are toggled an even number of times and end **off**. Only **perfect squares** have an unpaired divisor (√i), so they end **on**. The count of perfect squares up to n is ⌊√n⌋.

## Step by step

1. r = (int)Math.Sqrt(n).
2. Fix any floating-point error: while r² > n, r--; while (r+1)² ≤ n, r++ (in long).
3. Return r.
4. Which bulbs: 1, 4, 9, … up to n.

## Worked example

**Input:** n = 10

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| bulb 4 | divisors 1, 2, 4 → 3 toggles → on |
| bulb 6 | divisors 1, 2, 3, 6 → 4 toggles → off |
| on | 1, 4, 9 |

**Result:** 3

## Cost

O(1) for the count, O(√n) to list them.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Oct 2026) — LeetCode 319 Bulb Switcher.
// n bulbs start off. Round r (1..n) toggles every r-th bulb. Bulb i is toggled once per divisor of i, and
// divisors come in pairs except for perfect squares — so exactly the perfect squares end up on.
public static class BulbSwitcher {
    public static int OnAfter(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return 0;
        int r = (int)Math.Sqrt(n);                         // floor(sqrt(n)), corrected for floating-point error
        while ((long)r * r > n) r--;
        while ((long)(r + 1) * (r + 1) <= n) r++;
        return r;
    }

    // Follow-up: which bulbs are on (1-based)? The perfect squares up to n.
    public static List<int> OnBulbs(int n) {
        var on = new List<int>();
        for (long i = 1; i * i <= n; i++) on.Add((int)(i * i));
        return on;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why perfect squares?**

Divisors pair up (d, i/d); only a square has an unpaired divisor (√i), so an odd number of toggles.

**Q: Floating point?**

Math.Sqrt on a large int can round; correct it with integer checks.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(1) for the count; O(√n) to list them.

**Q: Shared state?**

None.

**Q: Only rounds 1..m, with m < n.**

Then bulb i's toggles = divisors of i that are ≤ m — no closed form; count with a sieve up to n.

**Q: How would you convince the interviewer?**

Simulate n = 1..10 on the board, notice 1, 4, 9 stay on, then explain the divisor pairing.
